import{c as t,S as s,j as o,m as a}from"./chunks/framework.C68YPdek.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"Cleaning up comments","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"developer/comment-cleanup.md","filePath":"developer/comment-cleanup.md"}'),n={name:"developer/comment-cleanup.md"};function i(r,e,l,h,d,c){return s(),o("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[a(`<h1 id="cleaning-up-comments" tabindex="-1">Cleaning up comments <a class="header-anchor" href="#cleaning-up-comments" aria-label="Permalink to “Cleaning up comments”">​</a></h1><p>This guide is for one job: going through existing code and removing comment clutter, in a pull request that changes comments and nothing else.</p><p>It applies to every Source Academy repository. Each repository&#39;s <code>AGENTS.md</code> has the short rules for writing new comments, and the facts that differ from repository to repository: which files are generated, and which commands to run. This guide says how to judge the comments that are already there. If a repository has no <code>AGENTS.md</code> yet, see <a href="#when-there-is-no-agents-md">When there is no <code>AGENTS.md</code></a>.</p><h2 id="what-a-cleanup-pull-request-is" tabindex="-1">What a cleanup pull request is <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-a-cleanup-pull-request-is" aria-label="Permalink to “What a cleanup pull request is”">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>Comment-only.</strong> No code changes, no renames, no reformatting.</li><li><strong>One file, or one folder, per pull request.</strong> Aim for at most about 150 comment lines changed, so that a person can read all of it.</li><li><strong>Not in generated files.</strong> The repository&#39;s <code>AGENTS.md</code> lists them.</li></ul><h2 id="when-there-is-no-agents-md" tabindex="-1">When there is no <code>AGENTS.md</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#when-there-is-no-agents-md" aria-label="Permalink to “When there is no AGENTS.md”">​</a></h2><p>Not every repository has one yet (this documentation repository does not). Then use these defaults, and say in the pull request which you used:</p><ul><li><strong>Generated files</strong> are those whose first lines say so, those in <code>.gitignore</code>, and those that <code>.gitattributes</code> marks <code>linguist-generated</code>. When in doubt, ask in the issue.</li><li><strong>Checks</strong> are the scripts of <code>package.json</code> (format, lint, test, build), or the commands in the README.</li><li>Writing the <code>AGENTS.md</code> is a good pull request of its own.</li></ul><h2 id="for-each-comment" tabindex="-1">For each comment <a class="header-anchor" href="#for-each-comment" aria-label="Permalink to “For each comment”">​</a></h2><p>Read the code first, then ask in this order. Stop at the first question that decides it.</p><ol><li><strong>Is it true?</strong> If it contradicts the code, fix it or delete it. A wrong comment is worse than none.</li><li><strong>Would a user of the package see it in their editor or in generated documentation?</strong> That is the comment of any exported symbol, and of any member of one: a method, property, enum member or parameter of an exported class, interface, type or enum. Keep it, and only fix the wording. This question comes before the next one, because such a comment often just says what the symbol is or does, and is still wanted.</li><li><strong>Does it say why?</strong> A reason, a constraint, an invariant, a workaround, a trap, the format of some data. If it only says what the next lines do, or tells the history (&quot;previously&quot;, &quot;used to&quot;, &quot;fixed per review&quot;), delete it.</li><li><strong>Is it as short as it can be?</strong> Shorten it to the reason. Drop issue and pull request numbers and the names of reviewers or review tools. Keep one <code>see #123</code> only if the issue holds context that the comment cannot carry.</li><li><strong>Is it in the right place?</strong> An explanation of a whole module&#39;s model belongs at the top of that module. Anything else longer than about ten lines belongs in a document, with a one-line pointer.</li></ol><p>When unsure, keep the comment and say so in the pull request.</p><h3 id="examples" tabindex="-1">Examples <a class="header-anchor" href="#examples" aria-label="Permalink to “Examples”">​</a></h3><p>These are from <code>py-slang</code>.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Comment</th><th>Decision</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>the 52-line header of <code>src/engines/py2js/runtime.ts</code> (<code>int -&gt; bigint</code>, <code>float -&gt; number</code>, <code>None -&gt; null</code>, and so on)</td><td>keep</td><td>it states an invariant that the whole file relies on</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;... <code>enclosingPreludeFunction</code> (py-slang#397). Unset for both native/bridged ...&quot;</td><td>keep the sentence, drop <code>(py-slang#397)</code></td><td>the explanation is the value; the number is history</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;... forever (issue #341). Populating the memo before descending means a ...&quot;</td><td>keep the sentence, drop <code>(issue #341)</code></td><td>it explains a trap (infinite recursion on cyclic data)</td></tr><tr><td><code>// String concatenation: str + str only.</code> above <code>if (typeof l === &quot;string&quot; || ...</code></td><td>check that it is true, then keep, fix or delete</td><td>question 1 comes first</td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="directives-are-code" tabindex="-1">Directives are code <a class="header-anchor" href="#directives-are-code" aria-label="Permalink to “Directives are code”">​</a></h2><p>Some comments are instructions to a tool, not text for people. Leave them exactly as they are: <code>eslint-disable...</code>, <code>@ts-expect-error</code>, <code>@ts-ignore</code>, <code>istanbul ignore ...</code>, <code>prettier-ignore</code>, <code>@jest-environment ...</code>, <code>#!</code> lines, license headers, <code>/// &lt;reference ...&gt;</code>, and the equivalents in other languages (for example Credo&#39;s <code># credo:disable-for-next-line</code>).</p><h2 id="never" tabindex="-1">Never <a class="header-anchor" href="#never" aria-label="Permalink to “Never”">​</a></h2><ul><li>Change code, rename anything, or reformat.</li><li>Delete a <code>TODO</code> that is still true. Open the issue and write <code>TODO(#123): ...</code>, or leave it as it is.</li><li>Delete the only record of why a workaround or a test exists. Shorten it instead.</li></ul><h2 id="before-you-open-the-pull-request" tabindex="-1">Before you open the pull request <a class="header-anchor" href="#before-you-open-the-pull-request" aria-label="Permalink to “Before you open the pull request”">​</a></h2><ol><li><p>The repository&#39;s format, lint and test commands pass (see its <code>AGENTS.md</code>).</p></li><li><p>The code is unchanged. For TypeScript, print every file without its comments, once on the base commit and once on your branch, and compare. The printer keeps types, interfaces and imports; compiling to JavaScript would drop them, and a changed type could go unnoticed:</p><div class="language-bash"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">bash</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark" style="--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark:#e1e4e8;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;--shiki-dark-bg:#24292e;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;"># on the base commit (&gt; /tmp/before.txt), then on your branch (&gt; /tmp/after.txt)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">yarn</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> node</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> -e</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> &#39;</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">const ts = require(&quot;typescript&quot;), fs = require(&quot;fs&quot;);</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">const printer = ts.createPrinter({ removeComments: true });</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">for (const f of process.argv.slice(1)) {</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">  const sf = ts.createSourceFile(f, fs.readFileSync(f, &quot;utf8&quot;), ts.ScriptTarget.Latest, true);</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">  console.log(&quot;//// &quot; + f + &quot;\\n&quot; + printer.printFile(sf));</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">}&#39;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> $(</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">git</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> ls-files</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> &#39;*.ts&#39;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> &#39;*.tsx&#39;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> &#39;*.mts&#39;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> &#39;*.cts&#39;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">) </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">&gt;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> /tmp/before.txt</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">diff</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> /tmp/before.txt</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> /tmp/after.txt</span></span></code></pre></div><p><strong>Open <code>/tmp/before.txt</code> first: if it is empty, no file was compared</strong> and the check proves nothing (the list above takes every tracked TypeScript file of the repository, not only those under <code>src</code>). Leave out the generated files that the repository&#39;s <code>AGENTS.md</code> lists, in both runs, with an exclude pattern such as <code>&#39;:!src/parser/python-grammar.ts&#39;</code> after the extensions.</p><p>No difference means that no code and no type changed. The printer normalizes whitespace, so a reformatting does not show here: the diff of your pull request does. The check does not cover directives (they are comments too), so leave those alone. A script that does the check for every language is planned.</p></li></ol><h2 id="the-pull-request" tabindex="-1">The pull request <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-pull-request" aria-label="Permalink to “The pull request”">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>Title:</strong> <code>Comments: &lt;path&gt;</code>.</li><li><strong>Body:</strong> the number of comment lines before and after; the long comments you kept, one line each on why; the comments you rewrote (before and after); the judgement calls a person should check; and &quot;No code changed&quot;, with the output of the check.</li></ul><h2 id="afterwards" tabindex="-1">Afterwards <a class="header-anchor" href="#afterwards" aria-label="Permalink to “Afterwards”">​</a></h2><p>If the same clutter comes back, make the rules in the repository&#39;s <code>AGENTS.md</code> sharper. Do not add to this guide.</p>`,25)])])}const m=t(n,[["render",i]]);export{u as __pageData,m as default};
